\subsection{Caveat}{

The 40-year remaining-life-expectancy assumption is a simplification: the
true conversion factor varies by age (a micromort taken late in life
converts to fewer microlives than one taken early in life, because there
is less remaining life expectancy to lose). \code{0.7} is therefore a
population-average approximation, not an individually-calibrated value.
This package has not been able to trace the exact \code{0.7} figure to a
verbatim formula in Spiegelhalter's original BMJ paper (the derivation
above is this package's own reconstruction, cross-checked against
independent secondary sources that report the same 40-year assumption and
arithmetic); the general micromort/microlife framework itself, however, is
directly attributable to that paper.
